      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "The best time to replace a roof in Utah is late spring through early fall (specifically fall being the gold standard), when temperatures are mild enough for asphalt shingles to properly seal and cure.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "late spring through early fall"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Why Timing Matters in Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Ideal Temperature Range (45\u00b0F to 85\u00b0F): Asphalt shingles rely on a thermally activated adhesive strip (sealant) that needs warmth and sunlight to bond the layers together.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Fall (The Gold Standard): Fall offers crisp, dry weather, moderate temperatures, and a chance to fortify your home before heavy mountain snows hit. However, contractors book up fast during this peak season.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Late Spring: Temperatures warm up (50\u00b0F\u201370\u00b0F) and winter snow has cleared, though you need to watch out for spring rain showers or late-season storms.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Summer Challenges: While days are long, intense summer heat can make shingles soft and gummy, and sudden afternoon thunderstorms can interrupt work.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Winter Challenges: Cold winter temperatures make shingles brittle and prone to cracking, and the adhesive sealant may not activate until the following spring, leaving your roof vulnerable to high winds.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              0,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Signs You Shouldn't Wait for \"The Best Season\""
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If your roof is actively leaking, sagging, or showing severe storm damage, waiting for the ideal season can lead to expensive structural water damage inside your home.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          1,
          6,
          7
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, let me know:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "What type of roofing material you currently have (or want)"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "The approximate age of your roof"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Whether you are dealing with active leaks or damage"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you figure out if you need an immediate fix or if you have time to wait for peak season."
      }
    ],
